  ],
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In a circuit shown in the figure, the capacitor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;is initially uncharged and the key <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> is open. In this condition, a current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> flows through the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#937;</mi></math> resistor. The key is closed at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. Which of the following statement(s)&nbsp;is(are) correct?</p><p>[Given : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>36</mn></math>]</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/ab0a548c-0db2-4253-b388-be88dde718f8.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 379px;\" /></p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "GV5R2",
      "text": "The value of the resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>&#937;</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "iHFWq",
      "text": "For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>, the value of current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "93XR2",
      "text": "At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>, the current in the capacitor is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "s6rxL",
      "text": "For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#8734;</mo></math>, the charge on the capacitor is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/95ff5d1a-e107-4cf1-a985-291f5f000c53.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 309px;\" /></p><p>From second branch, we can see the&nbsp;potential drop across the branch is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>.</p><p>Now for the first branch, we can write</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and from third branch, we can write</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p><p>Now from Kirchoff&#39;s junction rule,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p>Now, from equation(1), we can write</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>&#937;</mi></math></p><p>All three branches are in parallel, therefore we can write equivalent resistance as:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#937;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#937;</mi></math></p><p>As all branches are in&nbsp;parallel with the capacitor branch, potential drop across all three branches will be the same. Therefore,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/09e2376b-8e36-476a-a72e-1a04966c0430.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 204px;\" /></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Now, current variation in the circuit due to charging will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mfenced><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mstyle></mfenced></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></math></p><p>At,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;s</mi></math>, we get</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>18</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#215;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>30</mn><mn>18</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>30</mn><mn>18</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>36</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p><p>At steady state, voltage across capacitor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
